From: Q8B SteamOS port <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_bcm: keep DT max-speed and defer the baud switch

bcm_serdev_probe() discards bcmdev->oper_speed when the controller has no
reset/shutdown GPIO:

	if (!bcmdev->shutdown) {
		dev_warn(&serdev->dev,
			 "No reset resource, using default baud rate\n");
		bcmdev->oper_speed = bcmdev->init_speed;
	}

On the device-tree path init_speed is never populated (only the ACPI path
sets it), so oper_speed ends up 0 and the "max-speed" the DT asked for is
silently dropped.  On the Radxa Dragon Q8B this is not harmless: the link
stays at 115200, which makes powering the adapter on take ~0.43 s.  Steam
issues Set Powered=true and then StartDiscovery ~0.26 s later, so it gets
org.bluez.Error.NotReady and enters a power-cycle storm -- which shows up
in the Gamepad UI as a list that never refreshes, "Forget" that does
nothing and a "Paired" section that never appears.

Keep max-speed, but also defer the switch.  The early switch done by
hci_uart_setup() (hu->proto->set_baudrate, i.e. HCI_VS_Update_UART_Baud_Rate
0xfc18) runs before bcm_setup(), while the controller is still in its ROM
bootloader; the SYN43756B0 (AMPAK AP6276P) only accepts 0xfc18 after its
firmware has been loaded, so the early switch always times out:

	Bluetooth: hci0: command 0xfc18 tx timeout
	Bluetooth: hci0: BCM: failed to write update baudrate (-110)

Setting no_early_set_baudrate moves the switch into bcm_setup(), right
after btbcm_initialize() has loaded the firmware.  With that the link comes
up at max-speed and a warm power cycle drops from ~0.43 s to well under
Steam's 0.26 s window.

Signed-off-by: Q8B SteamOS port <noreply@localhost>
---
Local fix for the Radxa Dragon Q8B SteamOS port (AMPAK AP6276P /
BCM43756 over the SC8280XP GENI UART), not an upstream submission.

On-board evidence (2026-10-09):
  - warm power cycle at 115200 (hciconfig down/up): 0.430 s > 0.26 s.
  - early 0xfc18 switch (3 Mbps and 1.5 Mbps): tx timeout, link down.
  - the controller accepts 0xfc18 fine once the firmware is loaded.
---
 drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
index 1a4fc3882..1c41c7625 100644
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/hci_bcm.c
@@ -1534,9 +1534,13 @@ static int bcm_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 		return err;
 
 	if (!bcmdev->shutdown) {
-		dev_warn(&serdev->dev,
-			 "No reset resource, using default baud rate\n");
-		bcmdev->oper_speed = bcmdev->init_speed;
+		if (bcmdev->oper_speed)
+			dev_info(&serdev->dev,
+				 "No reset resource, keeping max-speed %u\n",
+				 bcmdev->oper_speed);
+		else
+			dev_warn(&serdev->dev,
+				 "No reset resource, using default baud rate\n");
 	}
 
 	err = bcm_gpio_set_power(bcmdev, false);
@@ -1553,6 +1557,17 @@ static int bcm_serdev_probe(struct serdev_device *serdev)
 			bcmdev->oper_speed = data->max_speed;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The SYN43756B0 (AMPAK AP6276P) only accepts
+	 * HCI_VS_Update_UART_Baud_Rate (0xfc18) after its firmware has been
+	 * loaded, so the early switch done by hci_uart_setup() -- before
+	 * bcm_setup() runs -- always times out.  Defer the switch into
+	 * bcm_setup() so the host UART is only moved once the controller is
+	 * actually listening on the new rate.
+	 */
+	if (bcmdev->oper_speed)
+		bcmdev->no_early_set_baudrate = true;
+
 	return hci_uart_register_device(&bcmdev->serdev_hu, &bcm_proto);
 }
 
